## Exclusive Write Access with &mut T

A **mutable borrow** (`&mut T`) gives read-write access to a value without taking ownership. Only ONE mutable borrow can exist at a time.

### Creating Mutable Borrows

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);
data.push(2);

let ref = &mut data;  // mutable borrow
ref.push(3);           // modify through borrow
// ref goes out of scope — borrow ends

data.push(4);  // OK — borrow ended
```

### &mut T in Function Parameters

```xiom
fn add_one(v: &mut Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  v.push(v.len());
}

fn double_all(v: &mut Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  var i = 0;
  while i < v.len() {
    v[i] = v[i] * 2;
    i = i + 1;
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1);
  double_all(&mut data);  // data now [2]
  add_one(&mut data);     // data now [2, 2]
  return data[0];
}
```

### Mutable Borrow Rules

1. **Exactly one**: only one `&mut T` at a time
2. **No shared borrows**: no `&T` while `&mut T` exists
3. **Original frozen**: cannot use original while mutably borrowed
4. **Exclusive access**: the borrower has exclusive read-write access

### &mut T vs &T

```xiom
var v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1);

// Shared borrow: read only
let r1 = &v;
let r2 = &v;  // OK — multiple shared borrows

// Mutable borrow: exclusive
let m = &mut v;
m.push(2);
// let r = &v;  // ERROR: cannot shared-borrow while mutably borrowed
```

### Mutable Borrow Scope

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);

{
  let m = &mut data;
  m.push(2);
  // m goes out of scope here
}

data.push(3);  // OK — mutable borrow ended
```

